23:28:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—and it perfectly exposes one of the most persistent myths in materials science. The short answer is: **glass is not a slow-flowing liquid**, so those cathedral windows have no reason to turn into puddles. They’ll hold their shape for a ridiculously long time. Here’s what’s actually going on. ### 1. Glass is an amorphous solid, not a liquid Glass is rigid and has a definite shape, but its molecules are arranged randomly rather than in a repeating crystal lattice. This “frozen liquid” structure led to the idea that glass might still be flowing, just extremely slowly. But at room temperature, the atoms are locked in place. It’s a solid—just one without long-range order, like a snapshot of a liquid’s chaotic arrangement. ### 2. The “thick at the bottom” myth comes from old manufacturing, not flow You’ve probably heard that medieval windowpanes are thicker at the bottom because the glass has flowed downward over centuries. In reality, those panes were made using the **crown glass process**, which produced sheets with natural unevenness—one edge was often thicker. When glaziers installed the panes, they deliberately placed the heavier, thicker edge at the bottom for stability. Some windows even have the thick part at the top or side, which would be impossible if gravity was pulling the glass down. ### 3. The viscosity is astronomically high Even if you insisted on treating glass as a liquid, its viscosity at room temperature is around **10²⁰ poise** (for comparison, water is about 0.01 poise). That means a glass window would need **billions of times the age of the universe** to sag even a millimeter. So no, a few centuries—or even a few million years—wouldn’t produce a puddle. The cathedral will crumble to dust long before the glass shows any flow. So rest easy: your local cathedral’s stained-glass saints aren’t slowly melting. They’re staying put, held up by solid (if disordered) glass and a healthy dose of human craftsmanship.
