23:29:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an *excellent* follow-up, and it gets right to the heart of the confusion. You’ve just named the one experiment that seems to prove that an apparently solid material can flow—so why not glass? The pitch drop experiment is indeed real, but the key is that **pitch and glass are in completely different physical states at room temperature**, despite looking similar. Here’s the crucial distinction. ### 1. The glass transition temperature is the dividing line Every amorphous material (one without a crystal structure) has a **glass transition temperature (Tg)**. Above this temperature, it’s a supercooled liquid and *can* flow, albeit extremely slowly. Below this temperature, it’s an amorphous solid—its molecules are locked in place, and flow essentially stops. - **Pitch (tar)** has a Tg that is *below* room temperature. At the ~20–25°C inside that Australian lab, pitch is actually a **liquid**—just an incredibly viscous one. That’s why the experiment works: it’s a liquid dripping, however slowly. - **Window glass (soda-lime-silica)** has a Tg around **500–600°C** (over 1000°F). At room temperature, it’s safely *below* its transition, meaning it’s a true solid. It doesn’t flow on any human timescale—or any timescale this side of the heat death of the universe. In other words, the pitch drop experiment demonstrates that an amorphous material can flow when it’s above its Tg. Glass at room temperature is not above its Tg; it’s deep in the solid regime. The same logic does *not* apply because the two materials are at fundamentally different points relative to their own glass transitions. ### 2. The viscosity numbers tell the same story You might want a quantitative comparison. The pitch in the famous experiment has a viscosity of roughly **2.3×10⁹ poise** (that’s 230 million times more viscous than water). At that viscosity, a drop forms in about 10 years. Ordinary window glass at room temperature has a viscosity around **10²⁰ poise**. That’s not just “a bit thicker”—it’s **over 100 billion times more viscous** than the pitch. To form a single drop, you’d need to wait something like **10³⁰ years**, which is trillions of times the age of the universe. Even if we heated a cathedral to the temperature of a hot oven (say, 200°C), the glass would still be a solid, no flow. You’d have to get it above about 500°C before it could start dripping—and at that point the cathedral would be on fire. ### 3. A thought experiment to seal the deal If window glass truly were like pitch at room temperature, we’d see the same effect in artifacts far older and thinner than cathedral windows. For instance, 2000-year-old Roman glassware would have sagged into puddles by now, yet delicate wine cups and intricate bottles hold their shape perfectly. The reason they survive is exactly the same: glass is a solid at normal temperatures, no matter how many centuries pass. So your analogy is brilliant—it’s just that you were inadvertently comparing a room-temperature liquid (pitch) to a room-temperature solid (glass). If you could somehow build a cathedral inside a blast furnace, then yes, the windows would eventually ooze. But then you’d have bigger problems than disproving a myth.
